// Headscale ACL policy — Phase 3 of docs/multi-site-migration.md.
//
// HuJSON (JSON with comments and trailing commas). Headscale reads this from
// disk with policy.mode = "file", which is why the overlay's policy lives in
// git and deploys with nixos-rebuild — the deciding difference against
// NetBird, whose policy lives in sqlite behind a REST API (overlay-evaluation
// §3.8).
//
// This is deliberately permissive between nodes, and that is a considered
// choice rather than a placeholder:
//
//   * The estate has exactly one human owner. There is no multi-tenant
//     boundary here for an ACL to enforce.
//   * From Phase 7 the overlay carries the *cluster's* traffic — etcd peer and
//     client (2379/2380), kubelet (10250), the apiserver (6443) and flannel
//     VXLAN (8472), between four nodes in three L3 domains. Enumerating those
//     port-by-port buys nothing while every node is equally trusted, and a
//     missing rule fails as an intermittent cluster fault rather than a clean
//     denial, which is a genuinely bad way to spend a debugging afternoon.
//   * The security boundary that matters is the *edge*: what the IONOS Cloud
//     firewall admits, and what Traefik terminates. Not peer-to-peer traffic
//     inside a mesh whose members are all ours.
//
// Tighten this when there is a second principal to separate — a family
// member's device, or a guest — not before.
//
// nodeAttrs — control-plane node attributes, separate concern from the ACLs
// above. `suggest-exit-node` is what the clients read (peer-side CapMap) to
// populate their exit-node menus: without it a peer advertising approved
// 0.0.0.0/0 routes is a fully functional exit node via the CLI
// (`tailscale set --exit-node=…`) while the macOS/iOS GUI shows an empty
// list — found on 2026-09-13 when maxdata joined ionos as an exit node
// (D18 follow-up) and appeared nowhere in the Mac's GUI. Headscale only
// surfaces the attribute on peers that advertise *approved* exit routes, so
// granting it tailnet-wide makes exactly ionos and maxdata visible. No
// client installs a default route without explicitly selecting an exit
// node (3.6.1), so this cannot silently reroute anyone.
{
  "acls": [
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["*"],
      "dst": ["*:*"],
    },
  ],

  "nodeAttrs": [
    {
      "target": ["*"],
      "attr": ["suggest-exit-node"],
    },
  ],
}
